<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kuttab</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as maktab, m'siid, mekteb</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A kuttab, or maktab, is a type of elementary school of the Muslim world, where a sheikh teaches a group of children seated before him on the ground. It taught reading, writing, grammar and Islamic studies, above all memorising and reciting the Qur'an, and often other subjects. Known since at least the 10th century and often attached to a mosque, it was described in the 11th century by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Ibn Sina</a>, who held that children should start at six and learn better in classes than with private tutors. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> kuttabs were often built above public water kiosks as sabil-kuttabs, and until modern schools spread in the 20th century they were the main means of mass education in much of the Islamic world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kuttab.jpg" alt="Kuttab" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cairo,_sabil_di_muhammad_'ali_pasha,_scuola.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In Arabic the word kuttab is used only of elementary schools. The same institution is called a maktab, or in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> an m'siid. In Persian it was the maktabkhana, a name also used in Azerbaijani, Ottoman Turkish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a>, and in Bosnian it is the mejtef or mekteb. The word kuttab shares its root, k-t-b, with kitab, &quot;book&quot;. In the medieval Islamic world an elementary school was known as a maktab, a term that dates back to at least the tenth century. Like the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a>, which provided higher education, a maktab was often attached to a mosque.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Ibn Sina</a>, known in the West as Avicenna, devoted a chapter of one of his books to the maktab in the 11th century, &quot;The Role of the Teacher in the Training and Upbringing of Children&quot;, as a guide for its teachers. He argued that children learn better in classes than from private tutors, citing the value of competition and emulation among pupils and of group discussion. He proposed that children enter at six and remain until fourteen, studying the Qur'an, Islamic metaphysics, language, literature, ethics and manual skills. After fourteen, pupils were to choose a subject of interest, such as medicine, geometry, trade or a craft, with the age of leaving kept flexible.</p><p>In the 16th century the jurist Ibn Hajar al-Haytami answered a petition from a retired judge who ran an elementary school for orphans. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatwa">fatwa</a> outlined a structure of maktab education meant to prevent any physical or economic exploitation of the enrolled orphans. Kuttabs were often built as parts of religious and charitable complexes sponsored by rulers or local elites. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> they were commonly set in a room above a sabil, a kiosk giving water to the public, in both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> and Ottoman Egyptian architecture. Ottoman külliyes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, including the Fatih Mosque and Süleymaniye complexes, contained a mekteb, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saadi-dynasty">Saadi dynasty</a> included an m'sid in complexes at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a> such as the Bab Doukkala Mosque and the Mouassine Mosque.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> had the highest literacy rate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, comparable to that of classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, and the maktab and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> were central to it. The kuttab came to represent an old-fashioned method of education in countries where most people are Muslim, in which a sheikh teaches students seated on the ground before him. Until modern schools developed in the 20th century it remained the prevalent means of mass education in much of the Islamic world.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kuttab” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They saw the traditional <b>maktab</b>, the primary school, as the clearest sign of stagnation.</blockquote><a href="/movement/jadidism">Jadidism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>They were staffed by secretaries (<b>kuttab</b>), skilled in accounts and in the intricate land tax, who saw themselves as the mainstay of the state and passed their knowledge from father to son.</blockquote><a href="/societies/abbasid-caliphate">Social Hierarchy of the Abbasid Caliphate</a></li><li><blockquote>Its scribes (<b>kuttab</b>), the “men of the pen”, staffed the chancery, which drafted the sultan’s letters and decrees, the bureau of the army, which kept the records of the amirs and their iqtas, and the treasuries.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mamluk-sultanate">Social Hierarchy of the Mamluk Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
